### Mass-scaling of GW170817B/GRB170817A

Specific to BH central engines of CC-SNe, the output (2) generalizes by mass-scaling M/M_{0}, satisfying

E \\simeq 3.5\\%\\left(\\frac{M}{M_{0}}\\right)M_{\\odot}c^{2}, \\Delta F \\simeq \\left(700-200\\right)\\left(\\frac{M_{0}}{M}\\right)\\mathrm{Hz}. 

Here, we assume a dimensionless Kerr parameter \\hat{a}\\simeq 0.8 similar to that of GW170817B/GRB170817A (van Putten, 2015; van Putten & Della Valle, 2023).

For instance, the distant DNS merger GW190425 (Abbott et al., 2020) during O3 points to a remnant of M=3.4\\,M_{\\odot}, suggesting considerable overlap with above-mentioned \\Delta F and B. On the other hand, the remnant of the candidate NS-BH merger GW190814 with M\\simeq 25.8M_{\\odot}(Abbott et al., 2020) suggests \\Delta F to fall below B, though still within the LVK bandwidth of sensitivity.

Energetic CC-SNe are expected to produce BHs with masses broadly correlated to their pre-collapse He-core mass. Their envelope-stripped and rotating progenitors have a mass-estimate of M_{He}(Kochanek, 2014). Combined with an estimated mass-fraction thereof collapsing to a BH, this shows the formation of rapidly rotating BH with M\\gtrsim 5M_{\\odot}(van Putten (2004) and references therein). Scaling (11) hereby predicts

E \\simeq 7\\%M_{\\odot}c^{2},\\ \\ \\ \\Delta F \\simeq \\left[100,380\\right]\\mathrm{Hz}. 

Fortuitously, \\Delta F in (12) and B in (10) overlap while E is substantially larger than the canonical output 2.5\\%M_{\\odot}c^{2} of GW170817 (Abbott et al., 2017a) giving a second gain factor of k_{M}\\simeq 2. This can also be seen from scaling of h_{char} in E_{\\mathrm{GW}} of (2) according to (Flanagan & Hughes, 1998; van Putten, 2001; Cutler & Thorne, 2002)

h_{char}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi D}\\sqrt{\\frac{E}{|\\Delta F|}}\\sim\\frac{M}{D}\\sqrt{ \\frac{\\mathcal{E}_{GW}}{M}}\\propto\\frac{M}{M_{0}}  for a source at distance D, where proportionality follows from (11). This, along with aforementioned improvement in detector sensitivity k_{D} over O2 presents a compelling outlook to probing the central engines of energetic CC-SN in the Local Universe during O4.

The horizon distance to (12) derives from a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in characteristic strain amplitude h_{char} of the source relative to the strain noise-amplitude of the detector. By numerical evaluation, Fig. 9 shows the gain in signal-to-noise by \\Delta F in (2) scaled by M/M_{0}. Energetic CC-SNe hereby receive a gain k_{n}\\gtrsim 1.27 in h_{char} for M\\gtrsim 5M_{\\odot}, shifting \\Delta F downwards to increasingly overlap with B in (10).

Combining the above, a total gain in horizon distance to energetic CC-SNe during O4 satisfies

k=k_{n}\\,k_{M}\\,k_{D}\\simeq 4.4. 

Fig. 6 shows the estimated horizon distance for CC-SNe as a function of a generic BH mass-range 3M_{\\odot}<M<5M_{\\odot}(e.g. MacCray, 1996). Including the estimate M_{0}\\simeq 2.8M_{\\odot} of GW170817B/GRB170817A, this extends to 2.8M_{\\odot}\\lesssim M<5M_{\\odot} with horizon distances 70\\lesssim D/\\mathrm{Mpc}\\lesssim 160.

## 6 Observational Outlook

The probability of observing GW-transients depends critically on local cosmic volume, V, proportional to the cube of the horizon distance, the total duration T of an observational run, duty cycle of the gravitational-wave detectors adjusted for data-quality (below) and any trend in source event rate with redshift (Aghaei Abchouyeh et al., 2023). SNe are generally more frequent with redshift, raising to a maximum around the epoch of maximal cosmic star formation (Melinder et al., 2012; Madau & Dickinson, 2014; Strolger et al., 2015). The probability of a detection hereby scales with at least VT, normalized to the local event rate at redshift zero.

Corrected for beaming (SS1), the true event rates of long and short GRBs are similar though not identical with different redshift distributions (van Putten et al., 2014). The former is indicative of the above-mentioned relatively small branching ratio (A1), leaving a true event rate of LGRBs of about 1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} within 100\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}.

Observing one GW170817-GRB170817A event during O2 and none during O3 is statistically consistent considering the detectors duty cycle and yield factors (Aghaei Abchouyeh et al., 2023). By the above-mentioned similar event rates of normal long and short GRBs, it is likewise consistent to have no GW-detection of a SN Ib/c during the runs O2-3.

The ground rule of a GW detection is a correlated response in two or more well-separated detectors. In more practical terms, we infer a GW detection by confirmation in at least two detectors operating at comparable performance. We quantify observational epochs accordingly by the _detector yield_y, the fraction of time when, for instance, both H1 and L1 are successfully operating simultaneously at nominal sensitivity (Aghaei Abchouyeh et al., 2023). The detector yield factor y for such joint operation varies with observational runs, reducing the effective observational time to Ty. A detailed study shows VTy for O2-3 to be comparable; the sensitivity of detectors in O3 improved over O2 but at the cost of a lower y for long-duration data segments lasting seconds (Aghaei Abchouyeh et al., 2023), relevant to our GW-signal of interest (2).

The observational run O4, recently starting its second half O4b, promises to be different, provided that the sensitivity is higher than that of O3 and the detector yield factor y of O4 is at least on par with O2, when y was relatively high by consistent joint H1L1-operations over time.

Probing CC-SNe for their central engines is to be carried out by un-modeled GW searches at _detector noise-limited sensitivity_ by time-symmetric search methods with equal sensitivity to ascending and descending GW-chirps (van Putten & Della Valle, 2023; Aghaei Abchouyeh et al., 2023). Ultra-long duration GW-emission may be produced by superluminous supernovae (van Putten & Della Valle, 2017).

Probing CC-SNe for (11-12) may be pursued in blind all-sky searches or in directed searches to specific events (van Putten, 2016). Notably, TOOs are provided by modern automated surveys such as the _Zwicky Transient Facility_(Graham et al., 2019) and accompanying high-energy EM emission by existing and planned satellite missions such as THESEUS (Amati et al., 2018). For the latter, only a small fraction of events may be producing a detectable GW-emission. While (A5) may be larger than (A1), most SN Ib/c may be unsuccessful in producing jets and breakout producing a (L)LGRBs. Indeed, most may be producing NS rather than BH. Even when producing a BH, they might be slowly rotating with insufficient spin-energy to match (10-11), or central engines with insufficient lifetime to allow jet breakout and a successful GRB, yet making a GW signal at (A5). Taking into account finite detector yield factors y, an EM-survey of \\sim 10^{3} SN Ib/c is required for a few hundred SN Ib/c to be probed by LVK for their GW-emission similar to (2).

### Gravitational wave search window

GW-emission of a CC-SN happens during the active lifetime of its central engine about the explosion time (t_{0}). SNe are serendipitously discovered (t_{O}) through their light curves hours to weeks after the shock breakout (t_{b}). This introduces

\\begin{split}\\Delta_{0}&=t_{b}-t_{0},\\\\ \\Delta_{1}&=t_{O}-t_{b},\\\\ \\Delta_{2}&=\\Delta_{0}+\\Delta_{1}=t_{O}-t_{0}.\\end{split} 

\\Delta_{0} can be estimated for different type of CC-SNe based on the radius of the progenitor star and the expansion velocity giving (Maurer et al., 2009; van Putten et al., 2011; Lyman et al., 2016; Sahu et al., 2009; van Putten et al., 2011; Taddia et al., 2019; Hamann et al., 2006; Sander et al., 2019; Gutierrez et al., 2017)

\\text{few hours}\\lesssim\\Delta_{0}\\lesssim\\text{several days}. 

A more precise estimation of \\Delta_{0} can be done for individual events. \\Delta_{1} is particularly uncertain, as it inevitably derives from extrapolating the SN light curve. In practice

\\Delta_{0}<<\\Delta_{1}. 

If we are lucky to detect the SN early enough, \\Delta_{1}\\lesssim 1d.

SN2011hd, a type IIb supernova in M51 (Arcavi et al., 2011), stands out as it was detected in X-rays. It signals a moment of discovery close to the shock breakout, whence \\Delta_{1}\\sim 12 hr (Fig. 5 of Soderberg et al. (2012)). If an event is observed well before the peak in the SN light curve and at high energy, \\Delta_{2} is estimated to be at least several hours, as the temperature of the shock front at t_{O} generally reduces after the peak. For instance, SN2023ixf and SN2024ggi light curves both point to similar \\Delta_{2}\\sim 1 d (Hosseinzadeh et al., 2023; Shrestha et al., 2024). Such timing reduces to (tens of) seconds only for directed searches to GRB-SNe, but these TOOs are exceptionally rare (SS1).

## 7 Discussion and Conclusion

CC-SNe are more promising sources for GW observation compared to DNS mergers because of their larger event rates SS1. Even core-collapse events that are not successful in GRB-emission, can potentially be loud in GW radiation (SS3). Accordingly, their event rate may well be a few times more numerous than that of DNS mergers. For BH central engines, calorimetry on the energy budget of different radiation channels shows that GW-emission may be dominant (3).

The above appears opportune for rotating BHs when spinning faster than the surrounding matter (Fig. 8). It should be emphasized that the duration of the output radiation can be long as it is determined by the lifetime of BH spin. This timescale readily covers seconds to tens of seconds (van Putten, 1999; van Putten & Levinson, 2003).

In surveying energetic CC-SNe in the Local Universe, some may reveal central engines of this kind. The recent observation of the wind precession of M87 and AT2020ocn lend credence to the exciting possibility of herein finding Lense-Thirring precession counterparts in gravitational radiation (SS4).

### Horizon distance to GW from CC-SNe

For the present LVK observational runs, we estimate a horizon distance of \\sim 160 Mpc to BH central engines of energetic CC-SNe (Fig. 6), based on the ample energy reservoir in angular momentum of rotating black holes of mass \\lesssim 5M_{\\odot}. For the observer, we introduce, an effective horizon distance corrected for the detector yield factor (Aghaei Abchouyeh et al., 2023) by

D_{eff}=y^{\\frac{1}{3}}D\\simeq[45-100]\\,\\mathrm{Mpc},  where D=[70-160] Mpc shown in Fig. 6 (SS5) is the horizon distance. This is based on O4 detector sensitivity, and detector yield factor y\\simeq 0.28 for long duration burst searches (the case of W=8 s in Aghaei Abchouyeh et al. (2023)) if similar to that of O2 and O3. This outlook may be contrasted with the galactic horizon distance (<1 Mpc) in conventional LVK searches for long duration GW-bursts (Abbott et al., 2016, 2020; Szczepanczyk et al., 2021). It accounts for the undesired bias in detecting compact binary coalescence (CBC) (Abbott et al., 2023) rather than observing extreme transient events more generally. (18) removes this bias through the open window offered by the broadband sensitivity of the LVK observatories in searches at detector-limited sensitivity also for un-modeled signals.

SNe Ibc and II event rates Cappellaro et al. (1999); Li et al. (2011); Cappellaro et al. (2015) show that there are about 100 and, respectively, \\gtrsim 200 events within 100 Mpc per year. Considering (A1)-(A2) and by (18), there are about 0.1-few LGRBs associated with these events (maybe unobserved due to beaming) within the horizon distance of D_{eff}. Including LLGRB-SNe in (A3), this fraction may increase by a factor of \\sim 10, increasing (L)LGRB events within D_{eff} that are potentially loud in GW. Accordingly by (A6) (which includes LGRBs, LLGRBs and failed GRBs), we expect >10 of SNe Ibc and II within the 100 Mpc per year to be targets of opportunity for aforementioned GW searches (GW-SN). These results are summarized in Table 2. The estimated rate of events interesting for gravitational radiation within the lower bound of 50 Mpc in (18) is therefore \\dot{N}_{\\rm GW-SN}\\times(50/100)^{(1/3)}\\gtrsim few per year.

This outlook derives from (11) and (14), upon scaling of GW170817B (2) by application of time-symmetric searches for un-modeled GW-signals at detector-limited sensitivity on par with CBC (van Putten & Della Valle, 2023).

Our estimated horizon distance is considerably beyond what may be expected in GW-emission in process of core-collapse, in the formation and evolution of rotating (proto-)NS (e.g. Lin et al., 2023). By their limited spin energy, the horizon distance to NS central engines is considerably smaller, especially at GW-frequencies about the LVK bandwidth of maximal sensitivity (10). Nevertheless, spin-down of proto-NS might be detectable at extra-galactic distances.

### Possible long duration burst GW search results

In light of the above, a probe of a directed search from a CC-SN within the 160Mpc, using a method with detector-noise limited sensitivity in the Local Universe may reveal the following for the central engine and remnant:

\\begin{table}

\\begin{tabular}{|c||c|c|c|c||c|} \\cline{2-6} \\multicolumn{1}{c|}{} & N (within 100 Mpc) & LGRB (true) & LLGRB (ture) & Failed GRB & GW-SN \\\\ \\hline SN Ibc & \\sim 100 & 1-3 & \\sim10 & few\\times LLGRB & few tens \\\\ \\hline SN II & \\gtrsim 200 & not observed & not observed & N/A & \\gtrsim 0 \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}

\\end{table}

Table 2: Number of CC-SNe Ibc and II within 100Mpc per year and their associated (L)LGRBs versus expected number of events that are loud in gravitational radiation. Crucially, there is window for a substantial fraction of GW-SNe amongst the failed GRB-SNe.

* **Case A.** No detection at low frequency (0-900Hz). Based on (11), this outcome rules out a BH central engine and points to an NS central engine, likely leaving an NS remnant and, accordingly, a progenitor mass below 20M_{\\odot}. Note that a BH remnant can not be ruled out in the exceptional case of a belated collapse to a BH. Regardless, a rapidly rotating NS central engine is expected to have its GW signature above 1kHz, well above the LVK bandwidth of maximal sensitivity (10).

* **Case B.** The detection of a GW-signal below 1 kHz but energetically below what is expected from (11). This may, instead, point to the spin-down of a slowly rotating proto-NS, pointing to an NS remnant and a progenitor mass below 20\\,M_{\\odot}. Alternatively, the signal is produced from a distant BH central engine which is not associated with the CC-SN at hand.

* **Case C.** The detection of a GW-signal below 1 kHz consistent with (11), evidencing a BH central engine, a BH remnant and hence a progenitor mass greater than 20M_{\\odot}. Possibly, this outcome would require corroborating evidence from the kinetic energy in supernova ejecta (Fig. 2) (van Putten et al., 2011). Notably, these signals may appear with characteristic side-bands (6) from disk precession (SS4).

Subject to the availability of simultaneous H1L1-data at nominal sensitivity, Case A-C provide radically novel observational constraints on the physical nature of the central engine, remnant, and the mass of the progenitor, otherwise notoriously challenging to determine by EM\\nu-observations alone.

Current targets of opportunities include SN II events SN2023ixf and SN2024ggi at \\sim 7Mpc, and the SN Ic events SN2018cow at 60 Mpc, SN2020bvc at 120 Mpc and SN2020oi at 23 Mpc (Kilpatrick et al., 2023; Xiang et al., 2021; Gonzalez-Diaz et al., 2024; Srivastav et al., 2024). These events can be probed at LVK detector-limited sensitivity for their un-modeled output in gravitational radiation by application of Butterfly Matched Filtering (van Putten & Della Valle, 2023). According to Case A-C, such is expected to conclusively break the degeneracy between an NS or BH central engine.

