## Appendix A Branching ratios of SN Ib/c

By their association to LGRBs, SN Ic-BL stand out as candidates for GW-SNe. However, the observed branching ratio of SN Ib/c to LGRBs, is quite small. We have

\\Gamma_{obs}=\\frac{N_{obs}^{\\prime}\\left[\\text{GRB-SN}\\right]}{N_{obs}\\left[ \\text{SNIb/c}\\right]}\\simeq 10^{-4} (A1)

This notoriously small ratio is due to the beaming factor f_{b}^{-1}\\sim 100 in GRB-emission (Frail et al., 2001; van Putten & Regimbau, 2003; Podsiadlowski et al., 2004; van Putten, 2004; Della Valle, 2006; Guetta & Della Valle, 2007; van Putten et al., 2019; Levinson & Nakar, 2020). Therefore, we have a true-to-observed event rate of GRB-SNe, satisfying (Woosley & Bloom, 2006; Hjorth & Bloom, 2012; Corsi et al., 2016; Ho et al., 2020; Corsi et al., 2023)

\\Gamma^{\\prime}=\\frac{\\dot{N}\\left[\\text{GRB-SN}\\right]}{\\dot{N}\\left[\\text{ SNIb/c}\\right]}\\simeq f_{b}^{-1}10^{-4}\\simeq 1\\%-3\\%. (A2)

This outlook is relevant to directed searches of gravitational-wave emission from the parent population of energetic SNIb/c. Consistently observational results show that SN Ic-BL are about 5% of the total population of SN explosions. In comparison, a tiny fraction of about 1% of these SNe are associated with LGRBs, which is about an order of magnitude less than LLGRBs (Corsi et al., 2023), though because of uncertainties these fractions are not well constrained (Corsi et al., 2016, 2023). Still, it can introduce an additional branching ratio

\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime}=\\frac{\\dot{N}\\left[\\text{LLGRB-SN}\\right]}{\\dot{N} \\left[\\text{SNIb/c}\\right]}\\simeq f_{bL}^{-1}10^{-4}\\simeq 10\\%. (A3)SN1998bw/GRB980425 is the first observation of a GRB associated with an SN, followed by more observations afterward. Recent estimations for SN1998bw like SN predicts their population to be 19% of total SN Ic-BL (Corsi et al., 2016, 2023).

Crucially, a successful GRB-SN requires the central engine to be long-lived for about ten seconds (van Putten, 2004, 2012; Nakar & Sari, 2012; De Colle et al., 2022). Shorter lifetimes are expected to produce LLGRBs or failed GRB-SNe with less-energetic jet or no jet breakout of the progenitor stellar envelope, the latter with unknown branching ratio

\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime\\prime}=\\frac{\\dot{N}\\left[\\text{failed GRB-SN}\\right]}{ \\dot{N}\\left[\\text{SNIb/c}\\right]}. (A4)

In defining (A4), we consider the failed GRB-SN events to be the continuation of successful (LL)GRB-SN originating from the same type of central engine, whereby \\Gamma^{\\prime}+\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime}+\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime\\prime}\\leq 1.

In the core collapse of massive stars, inevitably, the angular momentum of the central engine momentum will be broadly distributed with a positive correlation to lifetime and energetic output subject to efficiency. The more extreme cases of large angular momentum may support a successful GRB-SN, while less extreme may produce a SN sans GRB by insufficient lifetime for the above-mentioned successful jet breakout. Even such failed GRB-SNe still holds promise to be loud in GW-emission. Conceivably this extends to the case of negligible angular momentum failing to produce a central engine altogether, and hence no SN.

Illustrative for successful central engines is the rapidly spinning BH powering GW170817B/GRB170817A for the duration of 3.7 s (<10 s) (van Putten et al., 2019; van Putten & Della Valle, 2023). While \\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime} is not well-constrained, conceivably

\\frac{\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime\\prime}}{\\Gamma^{\\prime}+\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime}}= \\frac{\\dot{N}\\left[\\text{failed GRB-SN}\\right]}{\\dot{N}\\left[\\text{(L)LGRB- SN}\\right]}\\simeq\\text{few}. (A5)

Considering the diversity in the central engine of SN Ib/c, the fraction of all SNe Ib/c that are loud in GWs (GW-SN) hereby satisfies

\\Gamma_{GW}\\gtrsim\\Gamma^{\\prime}+\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime}+\\Gamma^{\\prime\\prime \\prime}\\gg\\Gamma_{obs}. (A6)

By the event rate of SN Ib/c in the Local Universe, (A6) points to an event rate of >10 GW-SN within 100\\,\\text{Mpc yr}^{-1}. In directed searches, SN Ib/c may hereby be significantly more attractive than (normal) long GRBs.

## Appendix B Formation and Evolution of BH Central Engines in CC-SNe

Following birth in core collapse of a massive star, a BH experiences three potential phases of evolution (Fig. 7; van Putten & Della Valle (2017)):

* _Opening:_ Initial surge of BH mass by in-fall of low specific angular momentum from the inner regions of the progenitor stellar envelope. This direct in-fall creates a rapid increase in mass without dramatic change in \\hat{a}. This specific angular momentum gradually increases as accretion continues, derived from the outer layers of the remnant stellar envelope. This surge, hereby, ceases when the critical value of the specific angular momentum defined by the ISCO of the BH is reached.

* _Middlegame:_ When a disk is formed, the BH may still continue to grow by Bardeen accretion of matter dripping across the ISCO on the viscous time scale of the newly formed accretion disk. This process may take the BH to a near-extremal state.

* _Endgame:_ A rapidly spinning BH with \\Omega_{H}>\\Omega_{T} may produce a suspended accretion state by a transfer of energy and angular momentum to the accretion disk via inner torus magnetosphere. This heats up the torus giving rise to multiple mass moments, allowing for a balance by cooling through the accompanying prodigious output in gravitational radiation. This state lasts for the lifetime of rapid spin of the BH.

The process of BH spin-down during the _Endgame_ is effectively described by (van Putten, 1999)

\\dot{M}=-\\Omega_{T}\\dot{J},\\,\\dot{J}=-\\kappa e_{k}(\\Omega_{H}-\\Omega_{T}), (B7) where J is the BH angular momentum, \\kappa parametrizes the interaction strength between the BH and surrounding matter with specific kinetic energy e_{k}(van Putten & Levinson, 2003; van Putten, 2015, 2016; van Putten & Della Valle,2017). The associated BH luminosity transferred onto the surrounding high-density matter is available for catalytic conversion into various radiation channels, described by the equations of suspended accretion (van Putten, 2001).

The BH luminosity is released predominantly in the form of gravitational radiation during the ensuing state of suspended accretion. This state lasts for the lifetime of BH spin, which scales inversely with e_{k} in (33). The output is accompanied by minor emissions in an ultra-relativistic baryon-poor jet emanating from the BH in its lowest energy state, and MeV-neutrinos and magnetic winds from the disk (3). GW luminosity of BH during the spin-down follows from the quadruple gravitational radiation formula (Peters and Mathews, 1963; van Putten and Sarkar, 2000; van Putten et al., 2019)

L_{GW}=\\frac{32}{5}\\left(\\frac{\\delta m}{M}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{zK_{GW}} \\right)^{5}L_{0}=2\\times 10^{51}\\left(\\frac{\\xi}{0.1}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\frac{ \\sigma}{0.01}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{zK_{\\alpha w}}\\right)^{5}\\mathrm{erg\\; \\;s^{-1}},  where, L_{0}=c^{5}/G, \\xi=\\delta m/M_{T} and \\delta m is the mass inhomogeneity in the torus created due to the heating. (33) and (34) present a 2+2 parameter space for Kerr BH evolution and its GW output, respectively defined by (M,\\ \\hat{a}=a/M) and (\\sigma,\\xi).

In this process, the spin of the BH decreases, the ISCO expands and, consequently, the radius of the torus increases (Fig. 8). The duration of this spin-down is determined by the lifetime of rapid spin of the BH (van Putten and Della Valle, 2017) that scales with the inverse mass-ratio of torus-to-BH, \\sigma=M_{T}/M,

T_{s}\\simeq 15\\,s\\left(\\frac{0.01}{\\sigma}\\right)\\left(\\frac{M}{7M_{\\odot}} \\right). 

Eventually, \\Omega_{H} reaches \\Omega_{T}, completing the _Endgame_ with a slowly rotating remnant (\\hat{a}\\simeq 0.36, Figs. 7-8).

